the unsupported response suggesting that  very strong inflammatory responses are  you know not good for uh for this  development of the antibody response we  also know that this is associated with  liver depletion and so perhaps  you lose a lot of your b cells and  plasma cells  um on the other hand we see that  basophil levels  both the cd16 expressing and then the  16 negative ones they are  positively associated with with the  optical response and that's been shown  also in other infections such as  influenza  that basophils can modulate and even  potentiate  b-cell responses so we find that to be  intriguing  to in 2019 as a final  section here final part of this analysis  was  we wanted to try to understand as i've  mentioned previously the natural course  of disease without any  immunomodulatory therapy patients were  also  not given antivirals in this study and  so this is really  representing the natural course when  patients are only treated by supportive  care  ventilatory support oxygen and so on  and what we were able to find was that  all of these patients  basically follow the same trajectory  there's clearly differences in the  speed the kinetics but also um  but the direction is pretty pretty much  the same  and one way to show that is what i'm  showing you here  what we're doing here is we're  integrating the measurements  of plasma proteins and cell populations  so both the old link data and the mass  cytometry data  and we're looking for shared latent  factors that explain  in this case changes over time  from acute to recovery  and we found that this latent factor too  is a is a factor that really  describes the changes that occur  from the acute phase to the recovery and  as you can see  all of the patients basically move  towards the top right corner where the  recovered patients sit and and  and so this we think gives us a  sort of integrated trajectory  that all patients need to follow during  their phase of recovery  and and what mediates this is  when we're looking at the loadings or  the proteins and cells  that change mostly along this latent  factor too  we see that this is primarily associated  with the decrease in the il-6 level  the levels of mcp3 and these other  plasma proteins such as ile  and so on that are listed here and  there's  it's also associated with an increase in  monocytes  both classical and non-classical as well  as  increase in eosinophils and gametophyte  cells  so they fulfilled as well so we  we think that this represents a  integrated trajectory of recovery  which might be possible to monitor  patients  to better understand whether a patient  is  on the verge of recovery or whether the  patient  is deteriorating in the disease  so this concludes the presentation with  the  of this study where we've performed the  systems level immuno monitoring analysis  from acute to recovery phase  of severe kobe 19 patients for more  details please  make sure to check out this preprint  that is on metaarchive  also i would like to i think we've  uncovered several co-regulated cell  populations and  and immune features over the course of  the disease  we've described some correlates of  productive antibody responses as well as  uncovered  a trajectory of recovery from acute  to recovery phases of the disease  um i would like to highlight all these  co-authors who have contributed to this  very quick  study both in my lab at the karolinska  institute  as well as the clinical center in  helsinki as well as the university of  health  so with that i'll thank you for your  attention and be happy to take any  questions
 

